00:00:08.910 --> 00:00:12.559
The purpose of the Panama Canal is pretty
easy to understand.

00:00:12.559 --> 00:00:14.809
It turns a journey of this into this.

00:00:14.809 --> 00:00:19.640
It brings the US west coast closer to Europe,
and it brings the US east coast closer to

00:00:19.640 --> 00:00:20.680
Asia.

00:00:20.680 --> 00:00:26.430
These rather short 180 foot or 55 meter wide
trenches are, combined, perhaps the single

00:00:26.430 --> 00:00:29.699
most important and most influential waterway
on earth.

00:00:29.699 --> 00:00:36.210
That’s because the entire shipping industry
worldwide is shaped by the Panama Canal.

00:00:36.210 --> 00:00:40.260
Hundreds of years ago, the opportunity of
a canal across the narrow country of Panama

00:00:40.260 --> 00:00:41.500
was identified.

00:00:41.500 --> 00:00:46.300
After a few earlier false starts, the Americans
went down to Panama in 1903, signed a fairly

00:00:46.300 --> 00:00:50.610
dubious treaty granting them land, dammed
up a river to make an artificial lake, dug

00:00:50.610 --> 00:00:54.570
a trench from that lake to the Pacific, dug
a trench from that lake to the Atlantic, and

00:00:54.570 --> 00:00:59.570
$13 billion and 5,600 worker fatalities later,
the canal was done.

00:00:59.570 --> 00:01:04.699
It officially opened the Pacific to the Atlantic
on August 15, 1914 as the first commercial

00:01:04.699 --> 00:01:07.400
ship, the SS Ancon, sailed through.

00:01:07.400 --> 00:01:11.560
Now, the Panama Canal is not just one straight
shot from ocean to ocean.

00:01:11.560 --> 00:01:16.570
That’s because the artificial lake they
created, Gatun Lake, is 85 feet or 26 meters

00:01:16.570 --> 00:01:17.860
above sea level.

00:01:17.860 --> 00:01:23.080
So, cargo ships, which can weigh more than
a billion pounds, need to get up those 85

00:01:23.080 --> 00:01:28.320
feet or 26 meters and then back down again
to transit from ocean to ocean.

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:33.060
Like most canals, to do this, the Panama Canal
uses locks.

00:01:33.060 --> 00:01:36.520
From the Atlantic side, for example, a ship
will be pulled into the first lock by the

00:01:36.520 --> 00:01:40.400
tug trains on each side of the canal, the
downstream gate will close, water from upstream

00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:44.460
will be fed in to fill the lock, the boat
will float up, then once the water level rises

00:01:44.460 --> 00:01:48.290
to match that of the next lock, the gate will
open, and the ship will be pulled into the

00:01:48.290 --> 00:01:49.479
next lock.

00:01:49.479 --> 00:01:53.690
Then, the gates will close, the water level
will rise again, the gates will open, then

00:01:53.690 --> 00:01:57.520
this will all happen one more time until the
third lock raises the ship to the water level

00:01:57.520 --> 00:02:01.320
of Gatun Lake—those 85 feet or 26 meters
above sea level.

00:02:01.320 --> 00:02:06.770
Believe it or not, it is these three locks
exclusively that limit what size a large portion

00:02:06.770 --> 00:02:09.110
of the world’s container ship fleet can
be.

00:02:09.110 --> 00:02:15.990
A ship has to be under 1,200 feet or 366 meters
long and 168 feet or 51 meters wide to be

00:02:15.990 --> 00:02:20.610
accepted in the Panama Canal and so a huge
proportion of the world’s ships are built

00:02:20.610 --> 00:02:24.270
to be exactly this size.

00:02:24.270 --> 00:02:28.930
This class of ship is called NeoPanamax or
New Panamax or Post Panamax depending on who

00:02:28.930 --> 00:02:30.160
you ask.

00:02:30.160 --> 00:02:34.150
Of course, there are certainly ships larger
than the NeoPanamax size.

00:02:34.150 --> 00:02:39.500
The Panama Canal has a size limit of about
15,000 TEU or twenty-foot equivalent units.

00:02:39.500 --> 00:02:43.920
That’s a measure of a ship’s capacity
based off how many of these twenty-foot containers

00:02:43.920 --> 00:02:44.970
they could carry.

00:02:44.970 --> 00:02:50.800
Now, the largest container ship in existence,
the OOCL Hong Kong, has a capacity of over

00:02:50.800 --> 00:02:53.050
21,000 TEU.

00:02:53.050 --> 00:02:57.730
This ship, like many of those larger than
15,000 TEU, exclusively keeps to the east

00:02:57.730 --> 00:03:01.810
Asia to Europe route as it can’t efficiently
make trips that require transit between the

00:03:01.810 --> 00:03:05.250
Pacific and Atlantic since it would have to
circumnavigate South America.

00:03:05.250 --> 00:03:09.610
But it was really not long ago that the largest
ships that could go through the Panama canal

00:03:09.610 --> 00:03:12.980
were far smaller than the current 15,000 TEU
size.

00:03:12.980 --> 00:03:19.640
You see, up until 2016, the Panama canal could
only fit ships smaller than 5,000 TEU.

00:03:19.640 --> 00:03:26.560
That’s because up until 2016, these didn’t
exist—the Panama canal’s new locks.

00:03:26.560 --> 00:03:31.480
The old locks can only only fit a ship 106
feet or 32 meters wide but still, given the

00:03:31.480 --> 00:03:36.670
enormous importance of this waterway, ships
were still then built specifically to fit

00:03:36.670 --> 00:03:37.670
through.

00:03:37.670 --> 00:03:43.049
In fact, 50% of the old lock’s users were
exactly as wide as this width limit suggested

00:03:43.049 --> 00:03:46.490
they were likely built for exactly that purpose.

00:03:46.490 --> 00:03:50.930
This was a problem for the Panama canal, though,
because, believe it or not, the canal has

00:03:50.930 --> 00:03:51.930
competitors.

00:03:51.930 --> 00:03:54.420
It all has to do with geography.

00:03:54.420 --> 00:03:58.040
When traveling from east Asia to the US east
coast, which is one of the busiest shipping

00:03:58.040 --> 00:04:03.689
routes in the world, it really is not all
that much further to go west than east.

00:04:03.689 --> 00:04:07.380
Going from Hong Kong to Newark, for example,
the distance heading west through the Indian

00:04:07.380 --> 00:04:12.040
ocean, Suez Canal, Mediterranean, and Atlantic
is only a few hundred miles or kilometers

00:04:12.040 --> 00:04:16.099
longer than going east across the Pacific
and through the Panama Canal.

00:04:16.099 --> 00:04:18.989
Meanwhile, the Panama canal is not cheap.

00:04:18.989 --> 00:04:24.419
They charge a $90 toll per twenty foot container
meaning that most ships, carrying thousands

00:04:24.419 --> 00:04:29.780
of loaded containers, pay hundreds of thousands
of dollars for just a single transit.

00:04:29.780 --> 00:04:33.870
With the new locks and their higher capacity,
single ships can possibly be assessed more

00:04:33.870 --> 00:04:38.110
than $1 million per transit if they’re the
largest size, fully loaded.

00:04:38.110 --> 00:04:41.370
The Suez canal, meanwhile, typically charges
lower tolls.

00:04:41.370 --> 00:04:45.820
A ship sailing that Hong Kong to Newark route
therefore might choose to take the slightly

00:04:45.820 --> 00:04:50.730
longer route via the Suez canal versus the
Panama canal as, in the end, when you consider

00:04:50.730 --> 00:04:53.759
the tolls, it might end up being less expensive.

00:04:53.759 --> 00:04:58.530
Now, both canals are constantly changing their
fees in order to compete with the other but

00:04:58.530 --> 00:05:04.380
still, throughout the past decade, the Suez
Canal seems to maintain a slight edge in cost.

00:05:04.380 --> 00:05:07.380
The Panama Canal’s other competitor, though,
is trains.

00:05:07.380 --> 00:05:12.381
For the crucial east Asia to US east coast
route, going through the Panama canal, while

00:05:12.381 --> 00:05:17.200
much faster than circumnavigating the continent,
is still not a direct route.

00:05:17.200 --> 00:05:22.610
On the Hong Kong to Newark route, the Panama
canal represents an almost 3,000 mile or 5,000

00:05:22.610 --> 00:05:27.710
kilometer diversion from the alternative—crossing
the US by land.

00:05:27.710 --> 00:05:32.360
It is pretty standard practice to ship cargo
from east Asia to Los Angeles and then load

00:05:32.360 --> 00:05:34.789
it up on trains to get to the US east coast.

00:05:34.789 --> 00:05:38.470
While overall this usually ends up a little
more expensive than going through the Panama

00:05:38.470 --> 00:05:44.590
Canal, it saves about a week in transit time
and, for many shippers, this is worth it.

00:05:44.590 --> 00:05:49.449
Trains, with their relatively low cost, represent
a considerable competitor to the canal, and

00:05:49.449 --> 00:05:53.770
it is the competition with both these and
the Suez canal that pushed the Panama Canal

00:05:53.770 --> 00:05:55.160
to make an expansion.

00:05:55.160 --> 00:06:00.530
That’s why they spent $5.4 billion to build
these bigger locks that could fit larger,

00:06:00.530 --> 00:06:02.569
15,000 TEU ships.

00:06:02.569 --> 00:06:06.370
This would let more of the world’s shipping
fleet through and these bigger ships, thanks

00:06:06.370 --> 00:06:10.330
to economies of scale, have lower cost per
container which helps make the canal more

00:06:10.330 --> 00:06:13.979
attractive in comparison the its trains or
Suez competition.

00:06:13.979 --> 00:06:18.370
Now, this expansion is a great example of
the butterfly effect.

00:06:18.370 --> 00:06:23.190
In one instance, because the Panama Canal
widened its size limit by a few dozen feet,

00:06:23.190 --> 00:06:28.840
the state of New Jersey had to spend $1.7
billion to raise a bridge by a few dozen feet.

00:06:28.840 --> 00:06:33.160
You see, in order to get to much of the port
of Newark, which is one of the largest and

00:06:33.160 --> 00:06:36.690
busiest in the US, ships have to pass under
the Bayonne bridge.

00:06:36.690 --> 00:06:40.790
As a huge proportion of the shipping traffic
to the US east coast goes through the Panama

00:06:40.790 --> 00:06:45.530
Canal, before the new set of locks many of
the ships that wanted to use the port of Newark

00:06:45.530 --> 00:06:48.830
were the smaller, 5,000 TEU size which could
all fit under the Bayonne Bridge, sitting

00:06:48.830 --> 00:06:53.009
at 151 feet or 46 meters above the water.

00:06:53.009 --> 00:06:56.650
With the canal expansion project, though,
more ships would start making their way to

00:06:56.650 --> 00:07:00.040
Newark that would be too large to fit under
the Bayonne bridge.

00:07:00.040 --> 00:07:04.680
Therefore, in order for the port of Newark
to stay competitive, they just had to raise

00:07:04.680 --> 00:07:05.759
that bridge.

00:07:05.759 --> 00:07:11.310
Beyond that, all up and down the US east coast,
from Miami to Savannah to Charleston to Baltimore

00:07:11.310 --> 00:07:16.090
and to New York, ports quickly spent millions
and billions of dollars to build facilities

00:07:16.090 --> 00:07:20.650
that would fit the bigger ships in time for
the opening of the new canal locks.

00:07:20.650 --> 00:07:24.730
Still today, though, many ports on the east
coast do not have the depth and facilities

00:07:24.730 --> 00:07:27.460
needed to handle these NeoPanamax ships.

00:07:27.460 --> 00:07:31.210
This, though, presents an opportunity for
the Caribbean.

00:07:31.210 --> 00:07:36.780
Deep-water ports in the Caribbean are developing
themselves into major transshipment hubs.

00:07:36.780 --> 00:07:41.650
What these are are ports where larger ships,
like the largest 15,000 TEU container ships

00:07:41.650 --> 00:07:45.849
that can now go through the canal, will unload
their cargo which will then be loaded again

00:07:45.849 --> 00:07:50.190
onto smaller ships bound for smaller ports
closer to the cargo’s destination up and

00:07:50.190 --> 00:07:51.660
down the east coast.

00:07:51.660 --> 00:07:56.300
Huge amounts of foreign money are flowing
into the Caribbean to develop these transshipment

00:07:56.300 --> 00:07:57.300
hubs.

00:07:57.300 --> 00:08:01.849
CMA CGM, a shipping company from France that
ranks as the world’s third largest, operates

00:08:01.849 --> 00:08:03.680
the port of Kingston, Jamaica.

00:08:03.680 --> 00:08:07.860
DP World, from the UAE, operates the port
of Caucedo in the Dominican Republic.

00:08:07.860 --> 00:08:12.460
Meanwhile, the port of Freeport, in the Bahamas,
is operated by Hutchison Port Holdings from

00:08:12.460 --> 00:08:13.610
Hong Kong.

00:08:13.610 --> 00:08:17.860
All of these companies and more have and continue
to focus on expanding these Caribbean ports

00:08:17.860 --> 00:08:21.570
to suit the increased traffic making its way
through the Panama Canal, into the Caribbean,

00:08:21.570 --> 00:08:25.070
and eventually to its destination on the east
coast.

00:08:25.070 --> 00:08:28.780
Sometime soon, though, something could happen
that would be simultaneously great for the

00:08:28.780 --> 00:08:31.620
Caribbean ports but terrible for the Panama
Canal.

00:08:31.620 --> 00:08:35.280
You see, two countries to the north of Panama
is Nicaragua.

00:08:35.280 --> 00:08:39.990
By linking both the Atlantic and the Pacific
to the large Lake Nicaragua, one could have

00:08:39.990 --> 00:08:42.649
another connection between the oceans.

00:08:42.649 --> 00:08:46.220
There has been a long history of attempts
to build this canal, most recently in the

00:08:46.220 --> 00:08:49.610
past decade with a failed scheme by a Chinese
billionaire.

00:08:49.610 --> 00:08:53.330
Given that this failure was rather recent
there are no current construction plans, but

00:08:53.330 --> 00:08:58.160
at least somewhere, the world is going to
need more canal capacity between the Pacific

00:08:58.160 --> 00:08:59.399
and the Atlantic.

00:08:59.399 --> 00:09:04.550
The shipping industry is growing at a solid
rate of about 5% annually and the Panama canal

00:09:04.550 --> 00:09:07.420
can only expand so much.

00:09:07.420 --> 00:09:11.050
One issue is that the Panama Canal could literally
run out of water.

00:09:11.050 --> 00:09:16.649
You see, each time the canal’s locks open,
millions of gallons of water flow out and

00:09:16.649 --> 00:09:18.010
downstream.

00:09:18.010 --> 00:09:21.820
Usually this is not a problem because the
lake upstream has enough water, but sometimes,

00:09:21.820 --> 00:09:26.430
during periods of reduced rainfall, there
just won’t be enough water to both release

00:09:26.430 --> 00:09:30.510
the amount needed to operate the locks and
keep the lake at a deep enough level for the

00:09:30.510 --> 00:09:31.550
largest ships to transit.

00:09:31.550 --> 00:09:36.899
At these times, which included much of Spring
2019, the canal will have to place restrictions

00:09:36.899 --> 00:09:41.250
on how much weight cargo ships can carry as
this will affect how deep they sit in the

00:09:41.250 --> 00:09:42.250
water.

00:09:42.250 --> 00:09:45.899
They’ve attempted to limit the effects of
this by installing systems to recapture some

00:09:45.899 --> 00:09:49.269
of the water used to operate the locks and
there are also potential plans to build an

00:09:49.269 --> 00:09:53.740
additional upstream dam to create an additional
reservoir, but these problems are only expected

00:09:53.740 --> 00:09:58.430
to worsen as climate change exacerbates the
severity of droughts in the region.

00:09:58.430 --> 00:10:02.200
All told, what happens in Panama affects the
world.

00:10:02.200 --> 00:10:07.149
If a drought in Panama restricts the throughput
of the canal, the world economy will feel

00:10:07.149 --> 00:10:08.149
it.

00:10:08.149 --> 00:10:11.940
That’s because shipping is what connects
the world’s economy and the Panama canal

00:10:11.940 --> 00:10:15.560
is one of the most important connectors in
shipping.

00:10:15.560 --> 00:10:20.190
The canal effectively brings the economies
on one side closer to those on the other and

00:10:20.190 --> 00:10:25.829
so, no matter what happens, it will continue
to serve as the world’s shortcut.

00:10:25.829 --> 00:10:30.050
Now, one of the things that those working
at the Panama Canal need to understand is

00:10:30.050 --> 00:10:31.550
how water works.

00:10:31.550 --> 00:10:36.370
As in, how weather works, how the water cycle
works, how tides work—all of these things

00:10:36.370 --> 00:10:41.150
are also, though, concepts that you can learn
about in Brilliant’s Physics of the Everyday

